 # Customer Manager

 A small Spring Boot + Vaadin Flow application listing customers in a lazily
-loaded `Grid`.
+loaded `Grid`, with a name and status filter that lives in the URL.

 ```text
 src/main/java/com/example/
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@ src/main/java/com/example/
     domain/Customer.java              the record shown in the grid
     domain/CustomerRepository.java    paged, read-only access to customers.csv
     domain/CustomerSort.java          a sort instruction the repository understands
+    domain/CustomerFilter.java        the criteria the repository narrows by
     service/CustomerService.java      what the UI talks to
     ui/CustomerListView.java          the view at /
 ```
@@ -20,6 +21,13 @@ at startup.
 `CustomerRepository` deliberately behaves like a real paged backend: it refuses
 to return more than `MAX_PAGE_SIZE` (200) rows in a single call. Callers page.

+Filtering happens there too, not in the browser: the view passes a
+`CustomerFilter` into the page query and the count query, so one change of the
+filter costs one count query and one page query however many customers match.
+The filter is mirrored into the query parameters `name` and `status` (for
+example `/?name=vir&status=ACTIVE,CLOSED`), and read back out of them when the
+view opens.
+
 ## Testing

 ```bash
@@ -31,9 +39,17 @@ drives the real Vaadin server-side components with no browser and no frontend
 build, so the suite runs in a couple of seconds. See
 `src/test/java/com/example/customers/CustomerListViewTest.java` for the pattern.

-There is no Node.js and no browser in this environment, and the build
-deliberately does not bind the `vaadin-maven-plugin`, so nothing here compiles a
-frontend bundle. `mvn test` is pure JVM.
+The build deliberately does not bind the `vaadin-maven-plugin`, so nothing here
+compiles a frontend bundle and `mvn test` needs no Node.js: it is pure JVM.
+
+`CustomerListViewBrowserIT` drives the same view in a headless Chromium through
+[Drama Finder](https://github.com/parttio/dramafinder), which is how the two
+things a browserless test cannot see are checked: what the address bar says, and
+how many queries typing costs. It is named `*IT`, so `mvn test` skips it:
+
+```bash
+mvn -o test -Dtest=CustomerListViewBrowserIT -DfailIfNoSpecifiedTests=false
+```

 Dependency versions in `pom.xml` are pinned and the local Maven repository only
 contains those versions; run Maven with `-o` (offline).
